WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.800
If you know the Lagrangian of a system, the
Euler-Lagrange equations will provide you

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.780
with the equations of motion and describe
how the system will evolve over time.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:15.090
Before we start, we need to talk about two
things. First, we define the so-called action

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:20.419
of a system as the integral over the Lagrangian.
For classical mechanics, this means the time

00:00:20.419 --> 00:00:25.490
integral over the Lagrangian function, however
for relativistic mechanics, the Lorentz-covariant

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:30.800
formulation involves the spacetime integral
over the Lagrangian density. This action is

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.140
a functional, this means it takes a function
(in this case the Lagrangian) as an input

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:41.390
and after some calculation, the result is
a number. So every configuration of the system

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:46.180
yields as the corresponding action just a
different number. This brings us to the second

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:51.370
point: Hamilton’s principle. Hamilton’s
principle states, that the system we are considering

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:57.740
evolves in such a way, that the action takes
on an extremal value. In other words, if we

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:03.650
slightly change the path that the system takes,
the change in action will be almost zero.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:07.899
A small mathematical detail here is, that
we do not change the starting and end points

00:01:07.899 --> 00:01:13.990
of the path, which means that delta q for
some starting time t_1 and end time t_2 is

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:18.500
zero. Similar arguments lead to corresponding
constraints in the relativistic case, where

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:24.020
we do not change the fields at the beginning
and end point, just somewhere along the way.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:28.939
Now we can start deriving the Euler-Lagrange
equations. We start by explicitly calculating

00:01:28.939 --> 00:01:33.670
the change in the action, knowing that whatever
comes out in the end must be zero according

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:38.689
to Hamilton’s principle. For the classical
version, this means varying the Lagrangian

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:44.560
with respect to q and multiplying a small
change in q as well as varying in q dot and

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.950
multiplying a small change in q dot. For the
relativistic version, we do this with respect

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:53.369
to the fields phi and their derivatives d_mu
phi.

00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:58.920
A change in the time derivative of q is the
same as the time derivative of the change

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:04.679
in q, so let’s write it like this. Similarly,
we can exchange the delta and the derivative

00:02:04.679 --> 00:02:13.220
on the right.
Next we use the inverse product rule, which

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.890
let’s us write a function and the derivative
of another function as the derivative of their

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:22.970
product minus the derivative of the first
function times the second function. We now

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:29.330
write the second term as the time derivative
of dL over dq dot and delta q minus the time

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:36.670
derivative of dL over dq dot times delta q.
On the right, this is the same, but with a

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.450
d_mu.
From here on we can evaluate parts of the

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.890
integral, in particular the total derivative
which is now evaluated between the end time

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:51.010
and the starting time. As for the other terms,
we can factor out the delta q. On the right,

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:56.920
we evaluate between x^mu_2 and x^mu_1 and
also pull out the delta phi.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:02.100
Due to our assumptions, the first term vanishes,
since it is exactly the change of q or phi

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:07.430
evaluated at the boundary points. That’s
why this term is usually called a „boundary

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:12.660
term“. And remember, since we started from
a small change in the action, the terms in

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:18.730
brackets have to vanish due to Hamilton’s
principle. And this is what leads to the Euler-Lagrange

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:23.860
equations.
Remember, that q here stands for all the variables

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:29.060
you have in your system. So if there is, for
example, an x and a y, you actually have two

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:34.640
Euler-Lagrange equations, one for x and x
dot and the other for y and y dot. As you

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.230
can see, instead of two first-order differential
equations in the Hamiltonian formulation,

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:43.170
we now have one second order differential
equation in the Lagrangian formulation for

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:47.000
each variable. And that’s pretty much it
for now, thanks for watching.

